<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maison Carrée</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maison Carrée is a Roman temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built under Augustus and dedicated around the turn of the era to his grandsons and adopted heirs Gaius and Lucius Caesar. It is one of the best-preserved Roman temples anywhere, a model of classical architecture that inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>, and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maison-carree.jpg" alt="Maison Carrée" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krzysztof Golik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maison_Carree_in_Nimes_(16).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
